# PART 8 — BUILD FILE AUTHENTICITY & FRAUD SIGNALS

Add a dedicated feature named:

# FILE AUTHENTICITY & FRAUD SIGNALS

This should be available within document analysis as its own section or tab.

It should analyse available file metadata and document-content warning signals.

It must be useful without an AI key.

AI may provide additional interpretation, but it must not be required for the basic checks.

## 8.1 Basic file analysis

Inspect:

- Actual file type and file signature where supported.
- File extension.
- MIME type.
- File size.
- Page count or image dimensions.
- Document metadata.
- Available creation/modification timestamps.
- Embedded metadata fields.
- Available author and software information.

Identify inconsistencies such as:

- File extension and actual file type mismatch.
- Missing or unusual metadata.
- Inconsistent file information.
- Unexpected document modifications, where evidence exists.
- Suspicious embedded links, where the existing parser exposes them.
- Unusual PDF structure indicators, where supported.

Do not treat a missing metadata field as proof of fraud.

## 8.2 PDF metadata

Extract available PDF metadata:

- Title.
- Author.
- Subject.
- Creator.
- Producer.
- Creation date.
- Modification date.
- Page count.
- Encryption status.
- Available signature information.

Distinguish document metadata from the computer's filesystem timestamps.

A file's modification timestamp does not prove when its content was created.

Do not claim that a PDF digital signature is valid unless its cryptographic validity and certificate chain have actually been verified.

If signature verification is not supported, only report that signature information was detected or was unavailable.

## 8.3 Image metadata

Use existing capabilities to inspect image metadata where available.

Potential signals:

- EXIF information.
- Camera/software information.
- Image dimensions.
- Creation timestamp.
- Editing software tags.
- Orientation information.
- Metadata inconsistencies.

Do not install a new EXIF package.

Use existing libraries or browser capabilities already present.

## 8.4 Text-based scam signals

Analyse the document text for suspicious patterns, including:

- Requests for unusual advance payments.
- Pressure to pay immediately.
- Unexpected bank-account changes.
- Unverified payment instructions.
- Suspicious links.
- Requests for passwords or OTPs.
- Unusual prize or guaranteed-return claims.
- Mismatched organisation identities.
- Unusual contact-information inconsistencies.
- Threatening or coercive language.

Every warning should include the supporting text and source location.

A warning must explain why the signal is suspicious.

## 8.5 Report design

Show findings in categories:

- File information.
- Metadata findings.
- File consistency checks.
- Document-content risk signals.
- Suspicious payment instructions.
- Link and contact warnings.
- Verification recommendations.

Use transparent labels:

- No warning detected.
- Informational.
- Low concern.
- Needs verification.
- Potentially suspicious.
- Strong warning signal.

A risk score may be used only if its calculation is transparent and based on actual findings.

Do not claim:

- "This document is definitely fake."
- "This is 100% genuine."
- "This image is AI-generated."

Metadata can be edited, removed or forged. A missing metadata field is inconclusive.

The feature must be an evidence-based warning system, not a fake authenticity certificate.

