# PART 13 — IMPORTANT PRODUCT BEHAVIOUR

The following principles must govern the entire application.

## A. Deterministic analysis must always be useful

Without an AI key, users should still receive:

- Extracted document text.
- Dates.
- Financial values.
- Document classification.
- Source-grounded key information.
- Requirements.
- Checklists.
- Tables.
- Evidence references.
- Basic fraud and metadata signals.
- Useful warnings.

AI is an enhancement, not a dependency.

## B. AI must improve understanding, not invent information

AI can:

- Explain difficult clauses.
- Summarise sections.
- Compare requirements.
- Identify relationships between information.
- Suggest questions to ask the issuing authority.
- Explain possible risks.
- Help users understand complex documents.

But AI must not invent the underlying facts.

## C. Every important result must be traceable

The user should be able to click a result and find where it came from.

A date, amount, eligibility condition or mandatory requirement without evidence must not be presented as confirmed.

## D. Different document types need different intelligence

Do not show the same generic report for every document.

Scholarships, RFPs, admissions, jobs, contracts, financial reports and general documents should use relevant extraction and analysis categories.

Use a general fallback for unknown document types without inventing category-specific information.

## E. Accuracy is more important than the number of results

Do not display 100 extracted findings if only 20 are meaningful.

Do not give a 100% completion score when important pages were not processed.

Do not show a huge checklist of invented requirements to make the application look powerful.

